macros.html.description:
In Rust, macros are a powerful metaprogramming feature that let you write code that writes code. They allow you to define patterns and generate code at compile time, which can reduce boilerplate, improve readability, or enable complex functionality.Metaprogramming
Concept in computer programming where, a program can treat other programs as input data
There are 3 kinds of macros in rust:The most straight forward macrosmacro_rules! say_hello { // Macros are created using macro_rules! macro () =&gt; { println!("Hello!"); };
} fn main() { say_hello!(); // Prints "Hello!"
}
These are more powerful and flexible, and work on Rust code as input (AST manipulation). They're like compiler plugins which generate code from macros.use proc_macro; #[some_attribute]
pub fn some_name(input: TokenStream) -&gt; TokenStream {
}
It prints the value of an expression along with the file name and line number where it’s used.fn main() { let a = 2; let b = dbg!(a * 3) + 1; dbg!(b);
} /* Output
[src/main.rs:4] a * 3 = 6
[src/main.rs:6] b = 7
*/
Can be passed written in the arms of a conditional where you are sure that the control won't reach to silence the return requirements by the compilerfn describe_day(day: u8) -&gt; &amp;'static str { match day { 1..=5 =&gt; "Weekday", 6 | 7 =&gt; "Weekend", _ =&gt; unreachable!("'day' should be between 1 and 7"), }
} fn main() { println!("{}", describe_day(2)); // Weekday println!("{}", describe_day(6)); // Weekend // println!("{}", describe_day(8)); // Panics with message
}
Crash the program, well its not a crash since the cleanup is done by unwinding the stack.panic!(":(");
